assertion  XSH.logb.001
anchor     functions/logb.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/logb.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1992
says       For x equal to R^k times a multiplier of 1 or 1.5 (negated or not),
           where R is FLT_RADIX, logb(x) is k: k is 0, 1, 3, 3, -2, -7, 5 and -3
           (multipliers below one are only used where they are exact powers of R,
           so the result does not depend on how the integral part of the
           logarithm is read for numbers below one).
test       t/001-values.c

assertion  XSH.logb.002
anchor     functions/logb.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/logb.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1992
says       logb(MIN/R), with MIN the smallest normal value of the type and R the
           radix, is the exponent MIN_EXP minus 2 when subnormal numbers exist.
test       t/002-subnormal.c

assertion  XSH.logb.003
anchor     functions/logb.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/logb.html#RETURN VALUE
class      disputed
since      1992
says       logb(0.375) is -2, the value for which 1 <= x * R^-logb(x) < R holds
           (the page also calls logb the integral part of the logarithm, which
           for -1.415 could be read as -1).
note       disputed (Q-103): the DESCRIPTION calls the exponent the integral part
           of log_r|x| and then gives an inequality that selects the floor of the
           logarithm; for |x| below one in the middle of a binade (0.375, log2 =
           -1.415) the two readings give -2 and -1. The test follows the
           inequality.
test       t/003-below-one.c

assertion  XSH.logb.004
anchor     functions/logb.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/logb.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1992
says       logb(+0) and logb(-0) return -HUGE_VAL (-HUGE_VALF, -HUGE_VALL).
test       t/004-zero-value.c

assertion  XSH.logb.005
anchor     functions/logb.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/logb.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     functions/logb.html#ERRORS
class      required
since      1992
says       logb(+0) and logb(-0) are pole errors, reported through errno (ERANGE)
           and the divide-by-zero exception as math_errhandling selects.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/005-zero-pole.c

assertion  XSH.logb.006
anchor     functions/logb.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/logb.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1992
says       A NaN argument gives a NaN result.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/006-nan.c

assertion  XSH.logb.007
anchor     functions/logb.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/logb.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1992
says       logb(+Inf) and logb(-Inf) return +Inf.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/007-infinity.c

assertion  XSH.logb.008
anchor     functions/logb.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/logb.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1992
says       With each rounding direction that can be selected (at least two are
           needed), logb(10) is 3 and logb(0.5) is -1 (a binary radix).
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard). The values are for a binary radix.
test       t/008-rounding-mode.c

assertion  XSH.logb.009
anchor     functions/logb.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/logb.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.23
class      required
since      1992
says       With errno holding EDOM before the call, logb(10) leaves it unchanged
           and, when exception flags are in use, raises none of the invalid,
           divide-by-zero and overflow exceptions.
test       t/009-no-error.c
